00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
 
this  is what the future of the british  military will look like  small highly trained teams working  across the globe  and better connected with each other  than ever before  they'll be equipped with a range of  small drones that can link  back to android screens on their chests  the screens can plot the position of  enemy  and friendly forces and be viewed in  real time  by commanders back in the uk it's game  changing  we will retire some capabilities we  don't need we recognize that some of the  new technology  uh you know means that you need less  people to do certain jobs that's just  the nature of weapon systems and  and and technology there will also be  a new special operations force called  the rangers  they will train foreign militaries and  then go  with them to the fight  you see the army operating across africa  particularly east africa will see the  army with a much more persistent  presence  uh in latin and south america and even  stretching as far afield  to the indo-pacific rim but  there will be a cost on monday afternoon  we will find out  what the trade-off is it is rumored that  the army will  shrink to 70 000 soldiers and the number  of tanks  will also be cut  the mod has been given more money to  spend by the prime minister  but some of that will have to pay for an  increase in the nuclear stockpile  the arrival of new threats does not mean  the old ones have gone away  i really want to see our country protect  itself from the full range  full spectrum of threats i'm afraid that  does require us to  increase our defense spending to at  least three percent  if we want to defend our security and  our economic interests  the defense chiefs and politicians  believe that with the right technology  they can do more with less  warfare is changing and so must the  british armed forces  alice debunked sky news bovington camp  in dorset

